/* data/learn-c/phy-12-06.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-06.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"A mechanical wave is a disturbance that travels through an elastic medium because its particles vibrate about their mean positions. Energy and momentum travel with the wave, but the matter of the medium does not. Electromagnetic waves need no medium. This chapter covers reflection of waves, superposition, stationary waves, vibrations of strings and air columns, beats and musical sound.","w":["A wave carries energy but not matter. Particles only move about their mean positions.","Mechanical waves need a medium; electromagnetic waves do not."],"x":["A mechanical wave is a disturbance that moves through an elastic medium, because the particles of the medium vibrate about their mean positions and pass the disturbance on to their neighbours. Energy and momentum travel with the wave, but the matter of the medium does not travel. Electromagnetic waves need no medium.","This chapter deals with the following. How a progressive wave is described, and what happens to it on reflection. What happens when waves meet (superposition), and how stationary waves are formed. The vibrations of strings and air columns, and how to find their frequencies. Resonance, beats, and the properties of musical sound.","Key quantities for every wave: amplitude A, wavelength λ, frequency n, period T = 1/n, and speed v = nλ. The speed depends only on the medium, and the frequency is set by the source."],"e":"A pebble dropped in a pond makes ripples that spread out. A leaf on the water only bobs up and down. So the wave carries energy outward, but the water itself does not move away with it."},"6.2":{"s":"A progressive (travelling) wave carries a disturbance steadily in one direction. Every particle repeats the same vibration with the same amplitude and frequency, but the phase changes from particle to particle. In a transverse wave the particles move at right angles to the direction of travel; in a longitudinal wave, along it. Longitudinal waves travel through solids, liquids and gases; transverse mechanical waves need a solid.","f":[{"x":"y = A*sin(k*x - omega*t)","n":"Wave travelling towards +x; k = 2 pi/lambda = wave number. For a wave towards -x use (k x + omega t).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.2, p.132"},{"x":"v = omega/k = n*lambda","n":"Wave speed; n = frequency = omega/(2 pi).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.2, p.132"}],"w":["The wave speed is fixed by the medium; the particle speed changes all the time during each vibration."],"x":["A progressive (travelling) wave carries a disturbance steadily in one direction. Every particle repeats the same vibration with the same amplitude and frequency. The phase changes from particle to particle, because each one starts a little later than the one before.","In a transverse wave, the particles vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel (waves on a string). In a longitudinal wave, they vibrate along the direction of travel (sound in air), forming compressions and rarefactions. Transverse mechanical waves need a medium with rigidity, so they travel in solids and on the surface of liquids, and not inside gases.","The equation of a wave travelling towards +x is y = A sin(kx − ωt), where k = 2π/λ is the wave number and ω = 2πn is the angular frequency. For a wave towards −x, use y = A sin(kx + ωt). The speed of the wave is v = ω/k = nλ.","Common mistake: mixing up the wave speed with the speed of a particle. The wave speed is fixed by the medium. The particle speed changes with time and is greatest at the mean position."],"e":"y = 0.02 sin(6.28x − 628t) in SI units. Here k = 6.28, so λ = 2π/k = 1 m. ω = 628, so n = 100 Hz. The wave speed is v = nλ = 100 m/s, towards +x."},"6.3":{"s":"At a boundary part of a wave is reflected. A transverse pulse meeting a denser medium (or a rigid end) comes back inverted: a crest returns as a trough, a phase change of π. From a rarer medium (or a free end) it returns the same way up. For a longitudinal wave, a compression reflected from a denser medium stays a compression; from a rarer medium it comes back as a rarefaction.","w":["Phase change of π on reflection only from a DENSER medium or a fixed end."],"x":["When a wave reaches the boundary of the medium, or enters another medium, part of it is reflected. The behaviour depends on the boundary.","Transverse wave, fixed end or denser medium: the reflected pulse is inverted. A crest returns as a trough, so there is a phase change of π. The fixed end cannot move, so it pulls back with an opposite force. Free end or rarer medium: the pulse is reflected without a change of phase, with the crest returning as a crest.","Longitudinal wave: reflection from a denser medium (or a closed end of a pipe) returns a compression as a compression. Reflection from a rarer medium (an open end) returns a compression as a rarefaction. The pressure change at a closed end is largest, and at an open end the pressure stays equal to that of the surroundings.","Remember the rule for transverse waves: a phase change of π happens only on reflection from a denser medium or a fixed end. The frequency, speed and wavelength of a reflected wave stay the same as those of the incident wave."],"e":"A pulse goes along a rope fixed to a wall. It returns upside down, as a trough. If the end of the rope is tied to a light ring that slides on a pole, the pulse comes back upright."},"6.4":{"s":"Principle of superposition: when two waves cross, each produces its own displacement as if the other were absent, and the actual displacement is their vector sum. The waves pass through each other unchanged. Two waves of the same frequency with a phase difference give a wave of the same frequency whose amplitude depends on that phase difference: largest in phase, smallest in opposite phase. Noise-cancelling headphones use this.","f":[{"x":"A = sqrt(A_1^2 + A_2^2 + 2*A_1*A_2*cos(phi))","n":"Resultant amplitude of two waves of the same frequency with phase difference phi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.4, p.136"},{"x":"I_max/I_min = (A_1 + A_2)^2/(A_1 - A_2)^2","n":"Intensity goes as amplitude squared: largest at phi = 0, least at phi = pi.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.4, p.136"}],"w":["Add the amplitudes, then square for intensity; do not add the intensities."],"x":["The principle of superposition: when two or more waves meet at a point, the resultant displacement is the vector sum of the displacements that each wave would cause alone. After meeting, the waves go on as if nothing had happened.","For two waves of the same frequency travelling the same way, with amplitudes A₁ and A₂ and a phase difference φ, the resultant has the same frequency and the amplitude A = √(A₁² + A₂² + 2A₁A₂ cos φ). The amplitude is the largest, A₁ + A₂, when the waves are in phase (φ = 0, constructive interference). It is the smallest, |A₁ − A₂|, when they are in opposite phase (φ = π, destructive interference).","Intensity is proportional to the square of the amplitude. So I_max/I_min = (A₁ + A₂)²/(A₁ − A₂)². First add the amplitudes, and then square. Do not add the intensities of the two waves to find the maximum.","Applications: noise-cancelling headphones produce a wave that is opposite in phase to the noise, and so cancel it. The same idea explains interference patterns of light and sound."],"e":"Two waves with A₁ = 3 and A₂ = 4 and a phase difference of 90° give A = √(9 + 16) = 5. In phase they give 7, and in opposite phase they give 1. The intensity ratio I_max : I_min is 49 : 1."},"6.5":{"s":"Two identical waves travelling in opposite directions along the same line, such as a wave and its reflection on a string, combine into a stationary (standing) wave. It does not move along: nodes stay at rest, antinodes vibrate with the largest amplitude, and the amplitude changes from point to point. All particles in one loop move in phase; neighbouring loops are opposite in phase. No energy is carried along.","f":[{"x":"y = 2*a*cos(2*pi*x/lambda)*sin(omega*t)","n":"Stationary wave; a = amplitude of each travelling wave. The amplitude 2a cos(2 pi x/lambda) depends on the position x.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.5, p.138"},{"x":"x = (2*p - 1)*lambda/4","n":"Nodes (p = 1, 2, 3 ...), measured from an antinode at x = 0; antinodes at x = p lambda/2.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.5, p.138"}],"w":["Node to node = λ/2; node to the next antinode = λ/4.","A stationary wave carries no energy along it; the energy stays in each loop."],"x":["When two identical waves travel in opposite directions along the same line, they combine to form a stationary (standing) wave. A common case is a wave on a string and its reflection from a fixed end. The pattern does not move along the string.","The equation is y = 2a cos(2πx/λ) sin ωt, where a is the amplitude of each travelling wave. The amplitude 2a cos(2πx/λ) changes with the position x. At the nodes, the amplitude is zero. At the antinodes, it is largest, equal to 2a.","Positions, taking an antinode at x = 0: antinodes at x = pλ/2 (p = 0, 1, 2, …), and nodes at x = (2p − 1)λ/4 (p = 1, 2, 3, …). Distance from node to node (or antinode to antinode) is λ/2. Distance from a node to the next antinode is λ/4.","Properties. All the particles in one loop (between two nodes) vibrate in phase, with amplitudes that depend on their position. The particles in the next loop are in opposite phase. A stationary wave does not carry energy along the string, as the energy stays in each loop. At a fixed end there is a node, and at a free end there is an antinode."],"e":"A string has a stationary wave of wavelength 0.6 m, with an antinode at x = 0. The nodes are at 0.15 m, 0.45 m, 0.75 m. The antinodes are at 0, 0.30 m, 0.60 m."},"6.6":{"s":"Free vibration: a body disturbed and left alone vibrates at its own natural frequency, and the amplitude slowly dies away. Forced vibration: an outside periodic force makes the body vibrate at the force's frequency; the closer that frequency is to the natural frequency, the larger the amplitude. When they are equal, the amplitude is largest: resonance.","w":["In forced vibration the body follows the DRIVER's frequency, not its own."],"x":["Free vibrations. A body that is disturbed and then left alone vibrates at its own natural frequency, which depends on its mass, shape and elasticity. In the real world the amplitude gradually falls, because of friction and air resistance. These are damped vibrations.","Forced vibrations. If a periodic outside force acts on a body, the body vibrates at the frequency of the force, not at its own natural frequency. The amplitude depends on how close the driving frequency is to the natural frequency. The closer they are, the larger the amplitude.","Resonance. When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, the amplitude becomes the largest, and the body takes in energy from the driver most efficiently. The amplitude is then limited only by damping.","Examples: a child on a swing pushed at the right moments; a tuning fork making a column of air in a tube sound loudly; a radio set tuned to a station. Dangerous resonance can occur in bridges and buildings, so soldiers break step on a bridge. Mistake to avoid: thinking that a forced vibration has the natural frequency of the body."],"e":"A tuning fork of 256 Hz is struck and its stem is held on a table. The table is forced to vibrate at 256 Hz, and sounds much louder than the fork alone. The table does not vibrate at its own frequency."},"6.7":{"s":"A string or air column vibrates only in modes fixed by its ends. The lowest is the fundamental (first harmonic); higher ones are overtones. A pipe closed at one end has a node at the closed end and an antinode just beyond the open end (end correction), so only odd harmonics occur. A pipe open at both ends and a string fixed at both ends give all harmonics. A string's frequency depends on length, tension and mass per length.","f":[{"x":"n = v/(4*(l + e))","n":"Pipe closed at one end, fundamental; e = end correction = 0.3 d, d = inner diameter. Only odd harmonics: n, 3n, 5n ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.142"},{"x":"n = v/(2*(l + 2*e))","n":"Pipe open at both ends, fundamental. All harmonics: n, 2n, 3n ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.144"},{"x":"n = (1/(2*l))*sqrt(T/m)","n":"String fixed at both ends, fundamental; T = tension, m = mass per unit length. All harmonics.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.7, p.147"}],"w":["For the same length, an open pipe's fundamental is twice a closed pipe's.","The p-th overtone of a closed pipe is (2p + 1)n; of an open pipe (p + 1)n. Overtones are not harmonics in general."],"x":["A string or an air column vibrates only in particular modes, which are fixed by its ends. The lowest frequency is the fundamental, or first harmonic. Higher frequencies are overtones. The first overtone is the next frequency above the fundamental.","String fixed at both ends: it has a node at each end, so l = λ/2, λ, 3λ/2, and so on. All harmonics are present: n, 2n, 3n. The fundamental is n = (1/2l)√(T/m), where T is the tension and m is the mass per unit length. The wave speed on the string is v = √(T/m).","Pipe closed at one end: the closed end is a node, and the open end is an antinode. Only odd harmonics occur: n, 3n, 5n, with n = v/4(l + e). The end correction is e = 0.3d for a pipe of inner diameter d. Overtones: first overtone 3n, second overtone 5n.","Pipe open at both ends: both ends are antinodes. All harmonics occur: n, 2n, 3n, with n = v/2(l + 2e). Overtones: first 2n, second 3n.","For the same length, the fundamental of an open pipe is twice that of a closed pipe. The p-th overtone of a closed pipe is (2p + 1)n, and that of an open pipe is (p + 1)n."],"e":"A closed pipe has a fundamental of 200 Hz. Its other frequencies are 600 Hz and 1000 Hz. An open pipe of the same length has a fundamental of 400 Hz, and also gives 800 Hz, 1200 Hz."},"6.8":{"s":"A sonometer is a hollow wooden box with a wire stretched over two movable bridges, kept taut by weights. The box amplifies the sound. The wire is set into vibration with a tuning fork; adjusting the bridges until a paper rider on the wire flies off finds resonance. It is used to check the laws of a vibrating string: frequency goes as 1/l, as √T and as 1/√m.","w":["At resonance the rider sits at an antinode, in the middle of the vibrating length."],"x":["A sonometer is used to study the vibrations of a stretched wire. It is a hollow wooden box, which amplifies the sound. A metal wire is stretched over two movable bridges, and the tension is set by a weight hung from one end.","Method for finding the frequency of a tuning fork: set the fork vibrating and hold its stem on the box. Place a small paper rider on the middle of the wire. Move one bridge until the wire is in resonance with the fork. At resonance the wire vibrates with a large amplitude, and the rider is thrown off. The vibrating length l is then measured.","The laws of a stretched string, tested with the sonometer: the frequency is inversely proportional to the length (n ∝ 1/l) when T and m are constant, directly proportional to the square root of the tension (n ∝ √T) when l and m are constant, and inversely proportional to the square root of the mass per unit length (n ∝ 1/√m) when T and l are constant.","At resonance the rider sits at an antinode, in the middle of the vibrating length, so it is thrown off. The wire and bridges must be clean, and the tension must be steady. Common mistake: measuring the length of the whole wire and not the length between the bridges."],"e":"A wire of vibrating length 50 cm is in resonance with a 256 Hz fork. If the length is made 25 cm at the same tension, the fundamental frequency becomes 512 Hz, as n is proportional to 1/l."},"6.9":{"s":"Two sound waves of the same amplitude and slightly different frequencies, heard together, rise and fall in loudness regularly: this is beats. One loud (waxing) and one soft (waning) make one beat. The number of beats per second equals the difference of the two frequencies. The ear can count beats only up to about 6-7 per second. Beats are used to tune instruments and to find an unknown frequency.","f":[{"x":"N = n_1 - n_2","n":"Beat frequency: difference of the two frequencies.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.9, p.151"},{"x":"T = 1/(n_1 - n_2)","n":"Time between two loud sounds (period of beats).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.9, p.151"}],"w":["Loading a fork with wax LOWERS its frequency; filing it raises it. Check whether the beats rise or fall."],"x":["When two sound waves with nearly equal amplitudes and slightly different frequencies n₁ and n₂ reach the ear together, the loudness rises and falls regularly. This effect is called beats. One loud sound followed by one soft sound is one beat. The sound is loud when the two waves are in phase, and soft when they are opposite in phase.","The number of beats per second (the beat frequency) is N = n₁ − n₂, the difference of the two frequencies. The time between two loud sounds is T = 1/(n₁ − n₂). The ear can follow beats only up to about 6 or 7 per second. At higher rates the sound seems rough.","Uses: tuning a musical instrument against a standard, by reducing the beats to zero; finding the unknown frequency of a fork.","Rule for an unknown fork: loading a fork with a little wax makes its frequency lower, and filing a prong makes it higher. If the beats decrease after loading, the unknown frequency is higher than the standard, and if they increase, it is lower. Common mistake: using the sum of the frequencies for the beat frequency."],"e":"A fork of 256 Hz gives 4 beats/s with an unknown fork. The unknown is 260 Hz or 252 Hz. After waxing the unknown, the beats fall to 2/s. Waxing lowers the frequency, so it moved nearer to 256 Hz. So the unknown is 260 Hz."},"6.10":{"s":"Sound has three characteristics. Loudness is how strong the sound feels; it depends on intensity and is measured on a logarithmic scale in decibels. Pitch depends on frequency: a higher frequency sounds shriller. Quality (timbre) depends on which overtones are present and how strong they are; it lets us tell two instruments apart at the same pitch and loudness.","f":[{"x":"beta = 10*log_10(I/I_0)","n":"Sound level in decibels; I_0 = 10^-12 W/m^2, the faintest audible intensity.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 6.10, p.153"}],"w":["Ten times the intensity adds only 10 dB."],"x":["Sound has three characteristics: loudness, pitch and quality. Loudness depends on the intensity of the sound, which is proportional to the square of the amplitude. It is the strength of the sensation in the ear. Pitch depends on frequency. A higher frequency has a higher pitch (a shriller sound). Quality (timbre) depends on the overtones present and on how strong they are. It lets us tell a violin from a flute playing the same note at the same loudness.","The ear responds to a very wide range of intensities, so the sound level is measured on a logarithmic scale in decibels (dB): β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀). Here I₀ = 10⁻¹² W/m² is the faintest sound that can be heard, and it corresponds to 0 dB.","Consequences of the scale: if the intensity becomes 10 times larger, the level rises by 10 dB. If it becomes 100 times larger, it rises by 20 dB. If it doubles, it rises by about 3 dB. Decibel values cannot be added: two 60 dB sources together give about 63 dB, not 120 dB.","Examples of levels: whisper about 30 dB, ordinary conversation about 60 dB, heavy traffic about 80 dB. Long exposure to sound above 85 dB can damage hearing."],"e":"Find the sound level of I = 10⁻⁶ W/m². β = 10 log₁₀(10⁻⁶/10⁻¹²) = 10 log₁₀(10⁶) = 60 dB."},"6.11":{"s":"Musical instruments use stationary waves. Stringed instruments (plucked, bowed or struck: sitar, violin, santoor, piano) use vibrating strings. Wind instruments (flute, harmonium, clarinet) use vibrating air columns. Percussion instruments (tabla, drum, cymbals) use stretched membranes or metal plates. The mix of fundamental and overtones gives each instrument its own sound.","w":["Closed pipes have only odd harmonics. Open pipes have all harmonics.","The note from a string depends on its length, tension and mass per unit length."],"x":["Musical instruments produce sound by stationary waves in strings, air columns, or membranes and plates. Each instrument gives a fundamental note and a mixture of overtones, and this mixture gives it its own quality.","String instruments: a vibrating string, set going by plucking (sitar, guitar), bowing (violin) or striking (piano, santoor). The pitch is changed by changing the length (pressing the string against the fret), the tension (tuning pegs), or by using strings of different thickness. A hollow body or sounding board increases the loudness.","Wind instruments: a vibrating column of air in a pipe (flute, clarinet, harmonium, trumpet). The pitch is changed by changing the length of the air column, with finger holes or valves. An open pipe gives all harmonics, and a closed one gives only the odd ones.","Percussion instruments: stretched membranes or metal plates, struck by hand or by sticks (tabla, drum, cymbals). Many of them do not give clear overtones, so their sound is less like a pure musical note."],"e":"Pressing a sitar string at a fret shortens the vibrating length. A shorter string has a higher frequency, since n is proportional to 1/l, so the note goes up. Tightening the peg also raises the note."}};
